sudo apt install git #No need to download the app Git, it can be installed to terminal.
git is a distributed version control system. git is almost always used as : git <subcommand> [flags] [arguments]
 #git — the binary #<subcommand> — what you want to do (commit, push, log, …) #[options] — flags that modify the subcommand (-m, --amend, -v, …)
 #[arguments] — targets (filename, branch, remote, …)
For more info: * git help <subcommand>  # full man page * git <subcommand> -h   # quick usage summary * git --help   # list common subcommands
 
Global flags (work with any subcommand). These go before the subcommand:
Flag	                Meaning
-C <path>     	    Run as if git was started in <path> (no cd needed)     *git -C ~/projects/app status  # run status in another dir
-c <key>=<value>	  Set a config value for this command only   *git -c user.name="Alice" commit -m "x"  # override config for one commit
--git-dir=<path>	  Point at a specific .git directory
--work-tree=<path>	Point at a specific working tree
--no-pager	        Don't pipe output through a pager  *git --no-pager log --oneline   # dump log without pager
-p / --paginate	    Force pager even for commands that don't page
--version	          Print git version
--help	            Print help
-v / --verbose	    More output

Subcommands grouped by purpose
A) Setup & configuration
Command	               What it does
git init	        Create a new repository (.git/ directory) *git init -b main 
git clone <url>  	Copy a remote repo locally
git config	      Read/write config (user, remotes, aliases, …)

Key flags with examples:
git init --bare                 # server-style repo with no working tree
git init -b main                # use "main" as the default branch name
git clone --depth 1 <url>       # shallow clone (only latest commit)
git clone --branch v1.2 <url>   # clone a specific branch/tag
git clone --recurse-submodules <url>
git config --global user.name "Alice"
git config --global user.email "alice@example.com"
git config --list               # show all config
git config --global alias.st status   # create alias: git st
--global = your user config, --system = machine-wide, --local (default) = current repo.

B) Inspecting state (read-only, safe)
Command         	  What it does
git status  	    Show working tree / staged changes
git log       	  Show commit history
git diff	        Show changes
git show	        Show a specific object (usually a commit)
git blame <file>  Show who last changed each line
git reflog	      Show where HEAD has been (lifeline for "I lost my commit") #commit is a saved instance of your work, a snapshot 

Key flags with examples:
git status -s                   # short format
git status -sb                  # short + branch info
git log --oneline               # one line per commit
git log --oneline --graph --all --decorate   # fancy ASCII tree
git log -n 5                    # last 5 commits
git log --author="Alice"
git log --since="2 weeks ago"
git log -p                      # show patches (diffs)
git log --stat                  # show file change stats
git diff                        # unstaged changes
git diff --staged               # staged changes (a.k.a. --cached)
git diff HEAD~1 HEAD            # diff between two commits
git diff main..feature          # diff between branches
git blame -L 10,20 file.txt     # blame only lines 10–20

Staging & committing
Command	          What it does
git add     	Stage changes for the next commit
git commit  	Record staged changes as a commit
git rm	      Remove files (and stage the removal)
git mv	      Rename/move files (and stage it)
git restore 	Discard changes in working tree or unstage
git reset	    Move HEAD / unstage (see below)

Key flags and examples:
git add file.txt                # stage one file
git add .                       # stage everything under cwd
git add -A                      # stage all changes (adds + deletes)
git add -p                      # interactively stage hunks (great tool)
git add -u                      # update tracked files only
git commit -m "message"         # commit with inline message
git commit                      # open editor for message
git commit -am "message"        # add tracked files + commit (skips `git add`)
git commit --amend              # rewrite the last commit
git commit --amend --no-edit    # amend without changing message
git commit -S                   # GPG-sign the commit
git commit --allow-empty -m "ci"  # commit with no changes (useful for triggers)
git rm file.txt                 # delete file + stage deletion
git rm --cached file.txt        # stop tracking, keep file on disk
git restore file.txt            # discard working-tree changes
git restore --staged file.txt   # unstage (keep changes in working tree)
Mental model: working tree → (add) → staging area (index) → (commit) → repository.

D) Branching & merging
Command	               What it does
git branch	     List/create/delete branches
git switch	     Change branches (modern)
git checkout	   Change branches or restore files (older, overloaded)
git merge	       Combine branches
git rebase	     Replay commits on top of another base
git cherry-pick	 Copy a specific commit onto current branch

Key flags and examples:
git branch                      # list local branches
git branch -a                   # include remotes
git branch -vv                  # verbose (upstream + last commit)
git branch new-feature          # create branch
git branch -d old-branch        # delete (safe)
git branch -D old-branch        # delete (force)
git branch -m new-name          # rename current branch
git switch main                 # change branch
git switch -c feature           # create + switch
git switch -                    # go back to previous branch
git checkout main               # old syntax for switch
git merge feature               # merge feature into current
git merge --no-ff feature       # always create a merge commit
git merge --squash feature      # squash into a single staged change
git merge --abort               # bail out of a conflicted merge
git rebase main                 # replay current branch on top of main
git rebase -i HEAD~3            # interactive rebase (edit/squash/reorder)
git rebase --continue           # after resolving conflicts
git rebase --abort              # cancel
git cherry-pick abc1234         # apply one commit
git cherry-pick abc1234..def5678  # apply a range

E) Remotes & syncing
Command         	What it does
git remote   	Manage remotes
git fetch   	Download objects/refs (does NOT touch working tree)
git pull	    fetch + merge (or rebase)
git push	    Upload local commits to a remote

Key flags and examples:
git remote -v                   # list remotes with URLs
git remote add origin <url>
git remote remove origin
git fetch                       # fetch from default remote
git fetch --all --prune         # fetch all + clean deleted remote branches
git pull                        # fetch + merge
git pull --rebase               # fetch + rebase instead of merge
git push                        # push current branch to its upstream
git push -u origin main         # push + set upstream (first time)
git push origin feature         # push specific branch
git push --force-with-lease     # SAFE force push (recommended)
git push --force                # DANGEROUS: overwrite remote
git push origin --delete branch # delete remote branch
git push --tags                 # push all tags
Rule of thumb: prefer --force-with-lease over --force. It refuses to overwrite if someone else pushed since your last fetch.

F) Undoing things (the "oh no" toolkit)
Command	           What it does
git restore	   Discard working-tree or staged changes
git reset	     Move HEAD; optionally reset index/working tree
git revert	   Create a new commit that undoes an old one (safe for shared history)
git clean	     Remove untracked files

Key flags and examples:
git reset --soft HEAD~1     # undo last commit, keep changes staged
git reset --mixed HEAD~1    # undo last commit, unstage but keep changes (default)
git reset --hard HEAD~1     # undo last commit AND discard changes (dangerous)
git reset file.txt          # unstage a file (mixed)
git revert abc1234          # new commit undoing abc1234
git revert HEAD             # undo the last commit
git clean -n                # dry run: what would be removed?
git clean -f                # remove untracked files
git clean -fd               # also remove untracked directories
git clean -fdx              # also remove ignored files (be careful!)
Golden rule: revert is public-safe (adds a commit). reset/rebase rewrite history — only do them on commits you haven't pushed, or use force-with-lease.

G) Stashing (temporary saves)
git stash                       # save working changes and revert to HEAD
git stash push -m "wip: auth"   # named stash
git stash -u                    # include untracked files
git stash list                  # see all stashes
git stash show -p stash@{1}     # diff of a stash
git stash pop                   # apply + drop top stash
git stash apply stash@{2}       # apply without dropping
git stash drop stash@{1}        # delete a stash
git stash clear                 # delete all stashes

H) Tags (releases)
git tag                         # list tags
git tag v1.0.0                  # lightweight tag
git tag -a v1.0.0 -m "Release"  # annotated tag (recommended)
git tag -d v1.0.0               # delete local tag
git push origin v1.0.0          # push a single tag
git push --tags                 # push all tags

I) Advanced / power-user
git bisect start                # binary search for the commit that broke things
git bisect bad                  # mark current as bad
git bisect good v1.0            # mark a known-good commit
git bisect run ./test.sh        # automate the search
git worktree add ../hotfix main # check out another branch in a new dir
git submodule update --init --recursive
git gc --aggressive             # garbage collect
git fsck                        # check repo integrity
git archive --format=zip HEAD > src.zip

4. The .gitignore and config files (worth knowing)
.gitignore — patterns for files git should ignore (node_modules/, *.log, .env)
.git/config — repo-local config
~/.gitconfig — global config
.git/HEAD — points to current branch

5. Common "instances" (real-world one-liners)
# First-time setup
git config --global user.name "Alice"
git config --global user.email "alice@example.com"
git config --global init.defaultBranch main

# Start a project
git init -b main
git add .
git commit -m "Initial commit"
git remote add origin git@github.com:alice/app.git
git push -u origin main

# Typical daily flow
git switch -c feature/login
git add -p
git commit -m "Add login form"
git push -u origin feature/login
# …open PR…

# Sync with main
git switch main
git pull --rebase
git switch feature/login
git rebase main

# Fix the last commit
git add forgotten.txt
git commit --amend --no-edit

# Recover after a bad reset
git reflog                      # find the lost commit hash
git reset --hard abc1234        # go back to it. 

